      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the olive oil in a large stockpot over medium-high heat. Brown the chicken thighs in batches, skin-side down first, for 4-5 minutes per side until golden. Remove and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Reduce the heat to medium. Add the sliced onions to the pot and cook for 10 minutes, stirring occasionally, until deeply softened and golden. Add the garlic, ras el hanout, turmeric, cinnamon and black pepper and cook for 1 minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Return the chicken to the pot. Pour in 1.2 litres of water and bring to the boil. Reduce to a gentle simmer, cover and cook for 40 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the red lentils to the pot and cook for a further 25-30 minutes until the lentils have dissolved into the broth and the chicken is falling off the bone."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Meanwhile, place the couscous in a large heatproof bowl. Pour over 360ml of boiling salted water, cover tightly and leave for 5 minutes. Fluff with a fork."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Taste the broth and adjust seasoning. Stir in the lemon juice. Spread the couscous in a large deep serving dish, ladle the broth generously over it and allow it to absorb for a couple of minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Arrange the chicken pieces on top. Scatter over the fresh coriander and parsley. Bring to the table and serve from the dish, making sure everyone gets some broth-soaked couscous, chicken and plenty of the fragrant broth."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The broth is too thick"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The red lentils will thicken the broth considerably as they dissolve. If it becomes too thick before serving, add another 200-300ml of water and bring back to a simmer to loosen it. The broth should be pourable and soupy, not a stew."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The couscous is absorbing all the broth before serving"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the broth over the couscous at the table immediately before eating rather than in the kitchen. Keep extra broth warm on the hob to offer alongside so people can add more as they eat."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The chicken skin is not browning"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pat the chicken dry with kitchen paper before browning. Wet chicken steams rather than browns. Make sure the pot is properly hot before adding the pieces, and leave them undisturbed for at least 4 minutes before attempting to turn them."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Rfissa is a celebration dish in Morocco, served at weddings and after childbirth. It takes time to make but most of the work is in the chicken braise. The shredded msemen or trid base soaks up the sauce. Fenugreek is the spice that makes it distinct. Use ras el hanout if you cannot find fenugreek seeds."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
